      "4": "They are called \"private parts\" for a few different reasons, mostly related to privacy, safety, and social rules.\n\n**1. Privacy and Boundaries**\nThe term \"private\" means something that belongs only to you. Because these parts of the body are involved in reproduction and sexual pleasure, they are considered very personal. Most people believe that these areas should be kept covered by clothing and only seen or touched by the person themselves, a doctor (during a check-up), or a partner in a consensual adult relationship.\n\n**2. Social Norms and Modesty**\nEvery culture has rules about what is appropriate to show in public. In most societies, covering the genitals is a standard rule of modesty. This helps maintain a sense of respect and boundaries between people in social settings, like at school, work, or in public places.\n\n**3. Safety and Consent**\nTeaching children that these are \"private parts\" is an important part of safety education. It helps children understand that their body belongs to them and that no one else should touch those areas without permission. This helps them recognize if a boundary is being crossed and encourages them to tell a trusted adult if something makes them feel uncomfortable.\n\nIn short, they are called \"private parts\" because they are personal, sensitive, and meant to be kept private from the general public.",
      "5": "**No, it is not okay for a friend to touch your private parts.**\n\nYour body belongs to you, and you have the right to decide who touches you. Here are a few important things to remember:\n\n*   **Your Boundaries:** You are the boss of your own body. If someone touches you in a way that makes you feel uncomfortable, scared, or confused, it is not okay.\n*   **The Rule of Privacy:** As we discussed, private parts are meant to be kept private. Friends, classmates, or other people your age should not be touching those areas, and you should not be touching theirs.\n*   **The Only Exceptions:** The only times it is generally okay for someone to see or touch your private parts are:\n    1.  **For health:** When a doctor or nurse is examining you to make sure you are healthy (and usually a parent or guardian is in the room with you).\n    2.  **For hygiene:** When a parent or caregiver helps a very young child clean themselves.\n\n**What to do if this happens:**\nIf a friend or anyone else tries to touch your private parts, or asks you to touch theirs, you should:\n\n1.  **Say \"No!\"** loudly and clearly.\n2.  **Get away** from that person as quickly as you can.\n3.  **Tell a trusted adult** immediately. This could be a parent, a teacher, a school counselor, or a relative.\n\nIt is very important to tell an adult you trust so they can help keep you safe and make sure it doesn't happen again. You will not be in trouble for telling the truth about something that made you feel unsafe."
