      "3": "\"Inappropriate\" is a big word that basically means **\"not right for the situation\"** or **\"not okay.\"**\n\nThink of it like this: wearing a swimsuit is appropriate at the beach, but it would be inappropriate to wear a swimsuit to school or to a fancy dinner. The swimsuit isn't \"bad,\" but it's just not the right thing for that specific place or time.\n\nWhen it comes to pictures and the internet, here is how you can tell if a picture is inappropriate to send:\n\n### 1. The \"Swimsuit Rule\"\nA simple way to remember is the **Swimsuit Rule**. \nAnything that is covered by a swimsuit is private. If a picture shows parts of your body that are usually covered by a swimsuit, it is **inappropriate** to send to anyone (except a doctor or a parent helping you stay clean/healthy).\n\n### 2. The \"Trusted Adult Test\"\nBefore you send a picture, ask yourself these three questions:\n*   **\"Would I be okay if my mom, dad, or teacher saw this picture?\"**\n*   **\"Would I be okay if this picture was shown to the whole school?\"**\n*   **\"Does this picture make me feel a little bit nervous or 'weird' inside?\"**\n\nIf the answer to the first two is **\"No,\"** or the answer to the last one is **\"Yes,\"** then the picture is inappropriate and you should not send it.\n\n### 3. The \"Pressure\" Test\nSometimes a picture might seem okay, but the *reason* you are sending it makes it inappropriate. \n*   **Appropriate:** Sending a funny photo of your dog to a cousin.\n*   **Inappropriate:** Sending a photo because someone is begging you, threatening you, or promising you something (like a gift or a game) in exchange for a photo. \n\n**If someone pressures you to send a picture that makes you feel uncomfortable, that is a big red flag.**\n\n### Summary Checklist:\nA picture is **inappropriate** to send if:\n*   It shows private body parts.\n*   You wouldn't want a trusted adult to see it.\n*   You are being pressured or tricked into sending it.\n*   It is meant to 