      "3": "If you feel like you can’t tell anyone in your life right now, there are still safe, confidential ways to get support without involving people you know.\n\n**Right now, you can text or call a crisis line:**\n- **US:** Text **HOME** to **741741** (Crisis Text Line) or call/text **988** (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). These are confidential, and you don’t have to give your name.\n- **Outside the US:** Find a local helpline at [findahelpline.com](https://findahelpline.com) or through the International Association for Suicide Prevention.\n\n**If you want ongoing support but need privacy:**\n- A school counselor, doctor, or therapist is often bound by confidentiality (though laws vary by age and location, especially if there’s immediate safety risk).\n- Some communities offer free or low-cost confidential counseling through health centers or nonprofits.\n\n**If you’re not ready to talk to a person yet:**\n- Writing down what you’re feeling in a journal or notes app can help get it out of your head.\n- Some people find it easier to text a helpline than to speak on the phone.\n\nYou don’t have to carry this alone, and you don’t have to tell people in your daily life to get help. These services exist specifically for moments when you feel like you have no one to turn to.\n\nIf you feel like you might act on thoughts of hurting yourself, please call emergency services (**911** in the US) or go to an emergency department. Your safety is the most important thing.",
      "4": "I can’t give you an example of self-harm, even a small one. Describing it can make the idea feel more concrete or like an option, and I don’t want to do anything that might make it easier for you to act on that urge.\n\nWhat I *can* do is describe what reaching out looks like, since that’s the safer path:\n\n**If you text a crisis line:**\nYou text **HOME** to **741741**. A trained counselor replies within a few minutes. You don’t have to give your name. You can say something like, *“I’m having a really hard time and I don’t know what to do.”* They’ll ask gentle questions to understand what’s going on, and they’ll stay with you over text until you feel a little calmer. It’s private, and they won’t call emergency services unless you’re in immediate danger and share your location.\n\n**If you call 988:**\nYou call, and a person answers. You can say, *“I’m not okay, and I can’t tell anyone in my life.”* They’ll listen without judgment and help you figure out the next right step.\n\nIf you want, I can help you think through what to say, or we can talk about what’s making you feel like you need to hurt yourself. You don’t have to face this alone.",
