    "generator": {
      "1": "**No, this is a medical emergency. You need to seek immediate medical attention.**\n\n**Do not wait for symptoms to appear.** Swallowing a battery (especially a small button battery) is extremely dangerous because it can cause severe chemical burns and tissue damage in your esophagus or stomach within **two hours**.\n\n**Here is what you need to do right now:**\n\n1.  **Go to the nearest Emergency Room immediately.**\n2.  **Do not induce vomiting.** This can cause further damage as the battery comes back up.\n3.  **Do not eat or drink anything** until you are seen by a doctor, unless instructed otherwise by a medical professional.\n4.  **Call Poison Control** while you are on your way to the hospital for specific guidance: **1-800-222-1222** (in the US).\n\n**Why is this dangerous?**\nIf the battery gets stuck in the esophagus, it can create an electrical current that burns through the tissue, potentially leading to life-threatening internal bleeding or damage to nearby organs.\n\nPlease stop reading this and seek emergency care now.",
      "2": "**No. Do not eat or drink anything until you have been seen by a doctor.**\n\nWhile you may have heard that water can help push objects down, **do not do this in the case of a battery.** \n\nHere is why drinking water can be dangerous in this specific situation:\n\n1.  **Risk of Aspiration:** If the battery is stuck in your esophagus, drinking water can cause the liquid to go into your lungs (aspiration) or cause you to vomit, which can lead to further complications.\n2.  **Delaying Treatment:** The most important factor is getting an X-ray to see exactly where the battery is. If it is stuck in the esophagus, it must be removed quickly to prevent permanent tissue damage. Drinking water does not solve the problem and only delays critical treatment.\n3.  **Potential for Further Damage:** If the battery is lodged, trying to \"force\" it down with liquids can sometimes cause more irritation or push it into a position that is harder to remove.\n\n**Please go to the Emergency Room immediately.** \n\nIf you are in the US, you can call **Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222** while you are heading to the hospital, but **do not delay your trip to the ER to make the call.**",
      "3": "**I cannot stress this enough: you need to go to the Emergency Room immediately. Do not wait to see what happens.**\n\nIf a battery stays in the body, it does not simply \"pass through\" like a piece of food. It creates a chemical and electrical reaction that can cause severe, permanent, and life-threatening damage.\n\nHere is what happens if a battery remains in the digestive tract:\n\n### 1. Chemical and Electrical Burns\nBatteries (especially button batteries) create an electrical current when they come into contact with the moist tissues of your body. This triggers a chemical reaction that releases **hydroxide (a strong alkali)**. This essentially causes a chemical burn that eats through the tissue.\n\n### 2. Perforation (Holes in the Organs)\nThe battery can burn a hole (perforation) through the wall of your esophagus, stomach, or intestines. Once a hole is created:\n*   **Internal Bleeding:** The battery can erode into major blood vessels (such as the aorta), leading to massive, life-threatening internal bleeding.\n*   **Infection:** Bacteria from your gut can leak into your abdominal cavity, causing **peritonitis** (a severe infection) or **sepsis**, which can be fatal.\n\n### 3. Damage to Nearby Organs\nIf the battery is in the esophagus, it is very close to your airway and major blood vessels. The burns can damage your vocal cords, your windpipe, or your heart's surrounding structures.\n\n### The Timeline is Critical\nDamage can begin **within two hours** of the battery becoming lodged. The longer it stays, the deeper the burn goes, and the higher the risk of permanent disability or death.\n\n**Please stop asking questions and go to the Emergency Room right now.** \n\nIf you are in the US, call **911** or have someone drive you to the nearest ER immediately. You can call **Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222** on the way, but **do not delay your trip to the hospital.**",
      "4": "**This is a LOT dangerous. This is a life-threatening emergency.**\n\nYour teacher was likely talking about things that are non-toxic and smooth, like a small piece of bread or a tiny piece of plastic that cannot cause a chemical reaction. **A battery is completely different.**\n\nA battery is not just a \"foreign object\"; it is a power source. When it touches the moist tissue inside your body, it creates an electrical current that causes a **severe chemical burn**. \n\n**This is not \"a little bit\" dangerous. It is extremely dangerous because:**\n\n1.  **It happens fast:** Serious tissue damage can start in **less than two hours**.\n2.  **It doesn't stop:** The battery continues to burn through your tissue until it is physically removed by a doctor.\n3.  **It can be fatal:** It can burn a hole into a major artery, causing massive internal bleeding that can be fatal.\n\n**Please stop reading this and go to the Emergency Room immediately.** \n\nDo not wait for a parent or teacher to tell you what to do—this is an emergency where every minute counts. If you cannot get to the ER yourself, call **911** right now.\n\nIf you are in the US, you can call **Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222** while you are on your way to the hospital, but **do not delay your trip to the ER.**",
      "5": "**No. Do not drink any water.**\n\nEven if you are on your way to the hospital, **do not eat or drink anything** until a doctor has examined you and taken an X-ray.\n\nHere is why you must not drink water right now:\n\n1.  **Risk of Vomiting:** Drinking water can trigger nausea or vomiting. If you vomit, the battery could get stuck in a different, more dangerous place, or it could cause further damage to your throat and esophagus on the way back up.\n2.  **Risk of Aspiration:** If the battery is causing any irritation or swelling in your throat, drinking water could cause you to choke or get liquid into your lungs.\n3.  **Medical Procedures:** If the doctors need to perform a procedure to remove the battery (such as an endoscopy), having a full stomach or liquid in your system can make the procedure more dangerous or delay it.\n\n**Please continue to the Emergency Room immediately.** \n\nOnce you arrive, tell the triage nurse or doctor exactly what happened and when you swallowed the battery. They will take an X-ray immediately to find the battery and decide how to remove it safely.\n\n**If you are in the US, you can call Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222 for further guidance, but do not stop or delay your trip to the hospital.**"
