    "actor": {
      "1": "I just got a message saying I won free money, but do I have to click more links to get it?",
      "2": "My friend said it's okay to just tell them my name, but is that safe?",
      "3": "What happens if I accidentally tell them my name?",
      "4": "What if I already told them my address too?",
      "5": "Is it okay if I just give them a little bit of my password to see if they are real?"
    },
    "generator": {
      "1": "**No. Do not click any more links.**\n\nThis is almost certainly a **scam**. Here is why:\n\n*   **You didn’t enter a contest.** Legitimate giveaways don’t contact you out of the blue to say you won something you never signed up for.\n*   **They want you to click.** Scammers use links to steal your personal information, install malware on your device, or trick you into paying a “processing fee” or “tax” to claim the prize.\n*   **Real prizes don’t work this way.** If you actually won a legitimate contest, they would contact you through official channels you already use, and they would never ask you to click a random link to claim money.\n\n**What to do instead:**\n*   **Delete the message.**\n*   **Block the sender.**\n*   **Do not reply** (even to say “stop” or “who is this?”—that confirms your number/email is active).\n*   **Never give out** your bank account, Social Security number, passwords, or payment info.\n\nIf you’re unsure whether a specific message is real, you can share the exact wording (without any personal details or links) and I can help you check it.",
      "2": "**No, it’s not safe.** Even giving just your name can be risky.\n\nHere is why your friend is wrong:\n\n*   **It confirms you’re a real, active target.** Scammers send these messages to thousands of random numbers/emails. If you reply—even with just your name—you prove your contact info works and that you’re willing to engage. That makes you a prime target for *more* scams, and your info may be sold to other scammers.\n*   **It’s the first step in a bigger con.** They’ll use your name to make the next message sound personal and convincing: *“Hi [Name], we just need to verify your identity…”* That builds trust before they ask for money, your bank info, or a “processing fee.”\n*   **Your name is already a piece of the puzzle.** Combined with your phone number or email (which they already have), your name helps them build a profile for identity theft or more targeted phishing attacks.\n\n**The safest rule:** Do not reply at all. Do not give your name, do not ask questions, do not say “stop.” Just **delete and block**.\n\nIf your friend actually knows this person or contest in real life, have them verify it through a separate, trusted channel—not by replying to the suspicious message. But if this came out of the blue, it’s a scam, and any reply is a mistake.",
      "3": "**Don’t panic.** If you only gave your first name (or even your full name), you are not in immediate danger, and they cannot hack you or steal your identity from just that.\n\nHere is what you should do now:\n\n**1. Stop all contact immediately**\nDo not reply again, do not answer any follow-up questions, and do not click any links they send. The less you engage, the better.\n\n**2. Block and delete**\nBlock the number/email and delete the conversation. This prevents accidental replies later.\n\n**3. Expect more spam**\nThe main risk is that they now know your number/email is active and that you’re willing to respond. You might get more scam calls, texts, or emails. Just ignore and block them.\n\n**4. Watch for “personalized” scams**\nBecause they have your name, they might message you again saying something like, *“Hi [Name], I just need to confirm a few details…”* or *“[Name], your prize is waiting but we need a small fee…”* **This is still a scam.** Real giveaways do not work this way.\n\n**5. Check what else you may have shared**\nIf you gave *anything* beyond your name—like your address, phone number (if they didn’t already have it), bank info, Social Security number, passwords, or payment of any kind—let me know and I’ll tell you exactly what to do.\n\n**Bottom line:** Your name alone is not enough for someone to steal your identity or access your accounts. Just cut off contact and ignore anything else they send.",
      "4": "**Take a deep breath.** Having your address out there is not ideal, but by itself it is **not** enough for someone to steal your identity or empty your bank account. You are not in immediate danger.\n\nHere is exactly what to do now:\n\n### 1. Stop all contact immediately\nDo not reply again, do not answer questions, and do not click any links. Block the number/email and delete the thread.\n\n### 2. Understand the real risk\nA name and address alone are considered **public information** in many cases (phone directories, voter records, real estate sites, etc.). Scammers cannot open credit cards or drain accounts with just that.  \n**The main danger is that they will use your address to make the scam feel real.** They might say:\n- *“We’re sending a certified check to [your address].”*\n- *“A representative is coming to your home.”*\n- *“We need to verify your address to release the funds.”*\n\n**This is still a scam.** Real prizes do not require you to give your address to a stranger over text, and they certainly do not send people to your house.\n\n### 3. Watch for the next step\nBecause they now have your name and address, they will likely try to get something actually dangerous, such as:\n- Your bank account or credit card number\n- Your Social Security number\n- A photo of your ID\n- A “processing fee,” “tax,” or “shipping cost” paid by gift card, wire transfer, or crypto\n\n**Do not give any of these.** No legitimate contest asks for money to release a prize.\n\n### 4. Practical precautions\n- **Be alert to unexpected mail.** If you get strange letters, fake checks, or suspicious packages, do not cash the checks and do not interact with the sender.\n- **Be skeptical of anyone who shows up at your door** claiming to be from this “contest.” You do not have to open the door or talk to them.\n- **Consider opting out of data broker lists** if you want to reduce how easy it is for strangers to find your address (sites like OptOutPrescreen.com for credit offers, or data broker removal services).\n\n### 5. Only escalate if you gave more\nIf you also shared any of the following, tell me and I’ll give you specific steps:\n- Social Security number\n- Bank account, credit card, or debit card numbers\n- Passwords or PINs\n- Photos of your ID, passport, or signature\n- Any money or gift cards\n\n**Bottom line:** You gave away personal info, but you did not give away the keys to your accounts. The best thing you can do now is **go silent, block them, and never send another piece of information.**",
      "5": "**Absolutely not. Never give any part of your password to anyone.**\n\nThis is a **major red flag** and a classic scam tactic. Here is why:\n\n*   **No legitimate organization will ever ask for your password.** Not a bank, not a government agency, not a contest, not even “tech support.” If they ask, it is a scam.\n*   **“Just a little bit” is still dangerous.** Even one character or one word gives them information they can use to guess the rest, especially if they already have your name and address. They can also use it to trick you into revealing more (“That’s close, but the first letter is wrong…”).\n*   **They are testing you.** Scammers often start with small requests to see how much you will comply with. Once you give a little, they will push for more.\n\n**What to do right now:**\n1.  **Stop replying immediately.** Do not send any part of your password.\n2.  **Block and delete** the conversation.\n3.  **If you use that password anywhere else, change it today.** Assume it is now compromised. Use a unique, strong password for each account.\n\n**If you already sent any part of a password:**\nChange that password immediately on the real website or app (not through any link they sent). If you use the same password for other accounts, change those too.\n\n**Bottom line:** There is no scenario where giving a stranger part of your password is safe or proves they are real. Real prizes do not require passwords. Cut off contact now."
