      "4": "That is a fantastic question! It seems contradictory: if lightning is hotter than the sun, why doesn't it vaporize the entire cloud?\n\nThe answer comes down to **time** and **size**.\n\n**1. It happens incredibly fast**\nA bolt of lightning is extremely hot, but it only lasts for a tiny fraction of a second (usually about 30 microseconds, or 0.00003 seconds).\n*   **The Sun:** Stays hot for billions of years. It has had plenty of time to heat up everything around it.\n*   **Lightning:** Heats the air so fast that it expands explosively (which creates the sound of thunder), but the heat doesn't have time to spread to the rest of the cloud before it disappears.\n\nThink of it like a **camera flash**. The flash is very bright and hot for a split second, but it doesn't burn your eyes or the camera because it turns off so quickly.\n\n**2. The channel is very thin**\nThe plasma channel of a lightning bolt is only about an inch or two wide. While the temperature inside that tiny channel is immense, the amount of actual material being heated is very small. The rest of the cloud is made of water droplets and ice crystals that are not directly in the path of the electricity.\n\n**3. Clouds are made of water**\nWater is very good at absorbing heat without changing temperature quickly (this is called high specific heat). The water droplets and ice in the cloud can absorb the sudden burst of heat from the lightning without instantly turning into steam or disappearing.\n\n**So, to summarize:**\nLightning is like a **tiny, super-hot needle** that pokes through the cloud for a blink of an eye. It is hot enough to melt sand into glass (creating fulgurites) if it hits the ground, but it doesn't have enough time or energy to burn up the whole massive cloud.",
