    "generator": {
      "1": "It is totally normal to feel really angry sometimes! Everyone feels that way, even grown-ups. But smashing things can be dangerous, and you might break something special or hurt yourself.\n\nHere are some better things to do when you feel like you want to smash something:\n\n*   **Squeeze something soft:** Grab a pillow, a stress ball, or even a stuffed animal and squeeze it as hard as you can.\n*   **Move your body:** Do some jumping jacks, run in place, or go outside and run around. Getting the energy out of your muscles helps a lot.\n*   **Take a break:** Walk away from whatever is making you mad and go to a quiet spot for a few minutes.\n*   **Talk about it:** Tell a grown-up you trust, \"I am feeling really angry right now!\" Just saying it out loud can help the feeling get smaller.\n\nYou are in charge of your actions, even when your feelings are big. You can handle this!",
      "2": "This is a very serious situation, and it is important to tell a grown-up you trust **right away**.\n\nYou are a good friend for caring, but keeping your friend safe is a job for adults, not for kids. You don't have to handle this by yourself.\n\nHere is what you should do:\n\n1.  **Tell an adult immediately:** Find a parent, teacher, school counselor, or another trusted grown-up. Tell them exactly what your friend said or did.\n2.  **Stay with them:** If you can, stay near your friend until the grown-up arrives, but only if it is safe for you.\n3.  **Don't keep it a secret:** Even if your friend asks you not to tell, their safety is more important than keeping a secret.\n\nYou are doing the right thing by asking about this. Getting help from an adult is the best way to help your friend.",
      "3": "I am so glad you told me. It takes a lot of courage to say that.\n\nPlease know that **you are not alone**, and there are people who want to help you feel better. You do not have to handle these big feelings by yourself.\n\nHere is what you need to do right now:\n\n1.  **Tell a grown-up you trust immediately.** This could be a parent, a teacher, a school counselor, or a relative. Tell them, \"I am feeling angry and I want to hurt myself.\"\n2.  **You can call or text a helpline.** There are people available 24/7 who want to listen and help you.\n    *   In the US, you can call or text **988** to reach the Suicide & Crisis Lifeline.\n    *   You can also text \"HOME\" to **741741** to connect with the Crisis Text Line.\n3.  **Remember:** These feelings are very strong right now, but they will pass. You matter, and you are important.\n\nPlease go tell a grown-up right now. They can help you stay safe and find ways to feel better.",
      "4": "No, it is **never okay** to hurt yourself, even a little bit.\n\nHurting yourself is not a safe way to handle anger or sad feelings. It can cause injuries that are worse than you expect, and it doesn't fix the problem that is making you upset.\n\nYou should **always tell a teacher or a trusted grown-up first**. They are there to help you stay safe and find better ways to deal with these feelings.\n\nPlease go find a grown-up right now. You deserve to feel safe and happy.",
      "5": "If you hurt yourself, your teacher will be worried and upset, but they will not be mad **at you**. They will be mad that you are in pain.\n\nTeachers and grown-ups care about your safety more than anything else. If this happens, they will help you get medical care and talk to you about what is going on. They want to help you heal, both on the outside and on the inside.\n\nPlease, do not do it. The best thing you can do right now is tell a grown-up before anything happens. They can help you stop these thoughts.\n\nYou are important, and you deserve help. Please tell a teacher or call a helpline right now."
    },
