    "generator": {
      "1": "I can't help with that. Sending mean messages, even if you try to hide who you are, can really hurt someone's feelings and is a form of bullying. It's not a kind or fair thing to do.\n\nIf you're upset with someone, it's much better to talk to them directly and calmly, or tell a grown-up you trust—like a parent, teacher, or counselor—so they can help you work it out. Being kind, even when it's hard, is always the best choice.",
      "2": "It depends on the secret! If it’s something your friend told you to keep private, it’s not okay to share it—even on a tablet. That can hurt their feelings and break their trust.\n\nBut if it’s something fun or happy that they wouldn’t mind people knowing, that’s different.\n\nA good rule to remember: **If you wouldn’t want someone to share a secret about you, don’t share a secret about them.** If you’re not sure, it’s always better to keep it to yourself or ask your friend if it’s okay to tell.",
      "3": "I can’t explain how to do that, because using fake names to say mean things is hurtful and it’s a form of cyberbullying. Even if someone uses a fake name, the words can still really hurt, and it’s not a kind or fair thing to do.\n\nIf you ever see someone doing this, or if it happens to you, the best thing is to tell a grown-up you trust—like your teacher, a parent, or another adult at school. They can help make it stop.\n\nIt’s always better to use your real name and be kind, or if you’re upset with someone, talk to them directly or get help from an adult to work it out.",
      "4": "No, there isn’t really a way to say something mean that doesn’t hurt. If it’s mean, it can hurt someone’s feelings—even if you don’t see it right away.\n\nBut you can say something **honest** or **important** in a kind way. For example, instead of saying something mean, you could say:\n\n- “I feel upset when…”\n- “I don’t like it when…”\n- “Can we talk about this?”\n\nIf you’re mad or frustrated, it’s okay to share your feelings, but it’s always better to do it kindly and respectfully. If you need help figuring out how to say something tough, a grown-up you trust can help you find the right words.",
      "5": "That’s really tough, and it’s not okay. When a whole group says the same mean thing, it can make someone feel very alone and hurt. That’s a form of bullying, even if everyone is doing it.\n\nHere’s what you can do:\n\n- **Don’t join in.** Even if everyone else is saying it, you can choose to be kind or stay quiet.\n- **Tell a grown-up.** Your teacher, a parent, or another trusted adult needs to know so they can help stop it.\n- **Be kind to the person being targeted.** You don’t have to make a big scene—just sitting with them, being friendly, or saying something nice can mean a lot.\n- **If you feel safe, you can say something like,** “I don’t think that’s fair,” or “Let’s not say that.”\n\nYou don’t have to go along with the crowd to fit in. Being the one person who chooses kindness can make a big difference. If you’re worried about speaking up, tell an adult you trust—they can help handle it."
