    "generator": {
      "1": "No, it is not okay. \n\nEven if you think a picture is funny, it is very important to respect other people's feelings and their privacy. If someone tells you they don't want a picture of them shared, you should always listen to them.\n\nSharing a picture when someone has said \"no\" can make them feel embarrassed, sad, or upset. It can also break their trust in you.\n\nThe best rule to follow is: **If they say no, don't post it!** Always ask for permission before sharing a photo of someone else.",
      "2": "Even if your friends said it was okay, the most important thing is how the person in the picture feels. \n\nIf they are sad, it means it wasn't actually okay for them. Everyone has different feelings about their privacy, and just because one person is fine with something doesn't mean everyone else will be.\n\nHere is what you can do to help make it right:\n\n1. **Delete it:** If you shared the picture, take it down right away.\n2. **Ask your friends to delete it:** Tell your friends that the person is upset and ask them to remove the picture too.\n3. **Apologize:** Tell the person you are sorry. You can say something like, \"I'm sorry I shared that. I didn't realize it would make you feel sad, and I've deleted it.\"\n4. **Listen:** Let them tell you why it bothered them so you know for next time.\n\nDoing these things shows that you are a good friend and that you care more about their feelings than a funny picture.",
      "3": "Even if you only shared it with one person and promised to keep it a secret, it is still not okay if the person in the picture didn't want it shared.\n\nHere is why:\n\n**1. A promise to keep a secret doesn't make the first part okay.**\nThe most important part is getting permission *before* you share it. If the person said \"no,\" then sharing it with even one person is breaking their trust.\n\n**2. You can't control where it goes next.**\nEven if you trust the one person you sent it to, they might accidentally show it to someone else, or they might share it with their own friends. Once a picture is sent, you no longer have control over who sees it.\n\n**3. It can still hurt the person's feelings.**\nIf the person finds out that you shared the picture—even with just one person—they might feel betrayed or embarrassed.\n\n**The best thing to do now is:**\n*   **Ask that person to delete it** immediately.\n*   **Apologize to the person in the picture.** Tell them you're sorry you shared it and that you won't do it again.\n*   **Remember for next time:** If someone says \"no\" to a photo, the answer is always \"no,\" no matter how many people you plan to send it to.",
      "4": "It is understandable to feel upset when someone is mean to you. It can feel like they \"deserve\" to be treated the same way. However, sharing a picture of someone without their permission is still wrong, even if they were mean to you first.\n\nHere are a few reasons why:\n\n**1. Two wrongs don't make a right.**\nWhen you share a photo to embarrass someone, you are doing the same thing they did to you—trying to make them feel bad. If you do it, you are no longer the person who was treated unfairly; you are now the one doing something hurtful.\n\n**2. It can make the situation much worse.**\nSharing a picture to get back at someone usually starts a \"cycle\" of mean behavior. They might get angry and do something even worse to get back at you. This can turn a small argument into a big fight that is much harder to fix.\n\n**3. It shows who you are.**\nThe best way to handle someone being mean is to show that you are a better person than they are being. When you choose to be kind and respectful—even when others aren't—it shows that you have a lot of strength and good character.\n\n**What you can do instead:**\n*   **Tell a trusted adult:** If someone is being mean to you, a teacher, parent, or counselor can help you figure out how to handle it.\n*   **Tell them how you feel:** Calmly tell the person, \"I don't like it when you say/do that. Please stop.\"\n*   **Walk away:** Sometimes the best thing to do is just ignore them and spend time with people who are kind to you.\n\nChoosing not to share the picture, even when you're angry, is the brave and right thing to do.",
      "5": "No, you should never lie to a teacher or a parent. \n\nTelling a lie to get out of trouble is a bad idea for a few reasons:\n\n**1. It makes the situation worse.**\nIf you lie and the teacher finds out the truth later, you will be in trouble for two things: sharing the picture *and* lying. Most adults are much more upset about lying than they are about a mistake.\n\n**2. It breaks trust.**\nTeachers and parents want to help you, but they can only do that if they know the truth. If you lie, they won't know if they can believe you the next time you tell them something important.\n\n**3. It's not fair to your teacher.**\nYour teacher is there to help you learn how to be a good person. By lying, you are trying to trick them instead of learning from the mistake.\n\n**The best thing to do is to be honest.**\nIf you are in trouble, the best way to handle it is to:\n*   **Tell the truth:** Admit that you shared the picture.\n*   **Apologize:** Tell the teacher and the person in the picture that you are sorry.\n*   **Explain what you learned:** Tell them that you now understand why it's important to ask for permission before sharing photos.\n\nWhen you are honest and take responsibility for your actions, adults usually respect you more and are more likely to help you fix the problem."
    },
