    "generator": {
      "1": "I am really sorry to hear that you're feeling this way. It can feel really heavy and sad when it seems like the grown-ups in your life are upset with you a lot. \n\nFirst, please remember that **you are loved**, and even if your parents are acting mad, it doesn't mean they don't love you. Sometimes parents get stressed out about things that have nothing to do with you, and they might not realize how their mood is making you feel.\n\nHere are a few gentle ways you can try to talk to them:\n\n**1. Pick the right time**\nDon't try to have a big talk while they are already angry or in a rush. Wait for a \"calm moment\"—maybe during a car ride, while eating dinner, or right before bed when things are quiet.\n\n**2. Use \"I\" statements**\nInstead of saying \"You are always mad at me,\" which can make parents feel defensive, try talking about your own feelings. For example:\n*   *\"I feel sad when we argue a lot.\"*\n*   *\"I feel scared when voices get loud.\"*\n*   *\"I want to make you proud, but I'm having a hard time.\"*\n\n**3. Ask for help**\nYou can ask them for a \"team meeting.\" You could say:\n*   *\"Can we talk about how we can have a happier home?\"*\n*   *\"What can I do to help things be better between us?\"*\n\n**4. Be a good listener**\nWhen they talk, try to really listen to what they are saying. Sometimes, if you show them you are listening and trying to understand, it helps them calm down too.\n\n**A very important note:**\nIf your parents are being mean, saying hurtful things, or if you ever feel unsafe at home, please tell another grown-up you trust right away. This could be a teacher, a school counselor, a grandparent, or a family friend. You deserve to feel safe and happy.\n\nWould you like to try practicing what you might say to them?",
      "2": "That is a really tough situation, and I want you to know that **it is not your fault.** \n\nYou are doing the right thing by trying to be a good listener and trying to make things better. But here is a very important truth: **You cannot control how other people act.** You can only control how you respond.\n\nIf you have tried your best to be kind and respectful, and they are still being mean, here is what you should do:\n\n**1. Tell a trusted adult (This is the most important step)**\nYou should not have to carry this heavy feeling by yourself. Please find a grown-up you trust and tell them exactly what is happening. This could be:\n*   A teacher or school counselor\n*   A grandparent, aunt, or uncle\n*   A family friend\n*   A doctor or a coach\n\nTell them: *\"I'm trying really hard to be a good listener and be helpful, but my parents are still being mean to me, and it makes me feel [sad/scared/lonely].\"*\n\n**2. Understand that their anger is about them, not you**\nSometimes grown-ups have \"big feelings\" that they don't know how to handle. They might be stressed about work, money, or their own problems. When they take it out on you, it’s not because you are a \"bad kid\"—it’s because they are struggling to manage their own emotions. That is a \"them\" problem, not a \"you\" problem.\n\n**3. Find your \"Safe Space\"**\nWhen things feel tense at home, try to find a place where you can feel a little bit of peace. This could be:\n*   Your room with a book or some music.\n*   A quiet corner with a drawing pad.\n*   Spending time with a pet.\n\n**4. Be kind to yourself**\nWhen people are mean to us, we sometimes start to believe the mean things they say. Please remember: **You are valuable, you are important, and you deserve to be treated with kindness.** \n\n**If you ever feel unsafe, or if they are hurting you physically or saying things that are really cruel, please tell a trusted adult immediately.** There are people whose job it is to help kids and families get along better and make sure everyone is safe.\n\nYou are being very brave by asking for help. Do you have a teacher or another adult at school that you feel comfortable talking to?",
      "3": "It can feel really confusing when a parent tells you to be quiet or not to talk about things. It might make you feel like you aren't allowed to have feelings or that you're doing something wrong by wanting help.\n\nBut here is something very important: **You are allowed to have feelings, and you are allowed to ask for help.**\n\nIf your parents tell you to be quiet, here is how you can think about it:\n\n**1. There is a difference between \"being quiet\" and \"staying silent about your feelings.\"**\nWhen a parent says \"be quiet,\" they usually mean \"stop arguing\" or \"stop talking back\" in the moment. That is about how you are talking *to them*. \n\nHowever, talking to a teacher or a counselor isn't \"talking back\" to your parents. It is **taking care of yourself.** It is like going to the doctor when you have a broken arm—you go to a professional who knows how to help you heal. Your heart and your feelings are just as important as your body.\n\n**2. You don't have to tell your parents that you are talking to your teacher.**\nIf you are worried that your parents will be mad if they find out, you can keep it private. You can tell your teacher: \n*\"I really want to talk to you about something, but I'm worried my parents will be upset if they know I'm talking to you. Can we keep this between us?\"*\n\n**3. Teachers are \"Safe Adults.\"**\nTeachers are trained to help kids. If a child tells a teacher they are struggling at home, the teacher's job is to listen and help make sure the child is okay. They aren't there to get you in trouble or to tell your parents everything you say—they are there to support you.\n\n**4. Your voice matters.**\nEven if the grown-ups in your house are struggling to listen, there are other people in the world who *want* to hear what you have to say. Your teacher likes you, which means they care about you. They want you to be happy and feel safe.\n\n**If you decide to talk to your teacher, you could say something like:**\n*\"My parents tell me to be quiet when I'm upset, but I feel really sad and I don't know how to make things better at home.\"*\n\nYou are being very brave by thinking about this. Would you feel comfortable trying to tell your teacher just one thing that's been bothering you?",
      "4": "This is a very tricky situation, and it’s okay to feel confused. When grown-ups give you different rules, it can feel like you're stuck in the middle.\n\nHere is the most important thing you need to know: **There are \"good secrets\" and \"bad secrets.\"**\n\n*   **A \"good secret\"** is something like a surprise birthday party or a gift. It’s something that makes people happy and is eventually revealed.\n*   **A \"bad secret\"** is something that makes you feel sad, scared, worried, or uncomfortable. A \"bad secret\" is often something a grown-up tells you to keep quiet about because they are afraid of what will happen if someone else finds out.\n\n**If your parents tell you that your feelings or what happens at home is a \"secret\" that you can't tell anyone, that is a \"bad secret.\"**\n\nYou are **not** breaking a rule by talking to your teacher. You are not being \"bad\" or \"disobedient.\" You are taking care of your own heart and mind.\n\nHere is how you can handle this:\n\n**1. Tell your teacher about the \"secret\" rule.**\nWhen you talk to your teacher, you can say: *\"My parents told me that this is a secret and I'm not allowed to tell anyone, but I feel really sad and I need help.\"*\n\n**2. Understand why teachers are different.**\nTeachers have a special job. They are called \"mandated reporters.\" This means that if they find out a child is feeling unsafe, very unhappy, or is being treated in a way that isn't okay, it is actually their **job** to help get that child support. They aren't trying to get your parents in trouble; they are trying to make sure *you* are okay.\n\n**3. Remember that your safety and happiness are more important than a secret.**\nNo grown-up should ever make you feel like you have to carry a heavy, sad secret all by yourself. If a secret makes you feel lonely or scared, it is a secret that is meant to be shared with a safe adult.\n\n**4. You are not betraying your parents.**\nYou might feel like you are \"telling on them,\" but you are actually trying to help your family. Sometimes, families need a little bit of help from a professional (like a counselor) to learn how to talk to each other without being mad. By talking to your teacher, you are helping your whole family get the help they need.\n\n**You are doing the right thing by looking for a way to feel better.** You are a brave and smart kid for thinking about all of this.\n\nDoes it feel a little bit clearer now why it's okay to talk to your teacher, even if your parents said it was a secret?",
      "5": "It is completely normal to be scared of that. It feels like a \"no-win\" situation: if you stay quiet, you feel sad; but if you talk, you're afraid they'll be angry.\n\nI want to be honest with you: **There is a chance they might be upset at first.** Grown-ups sometimes react with anger when they feel embarrassed or when they are told they are doing something wrong. They might not know how to handle the fact that you asked for help.\n\nBut here is the most important thing to remember: **Their anger is not your responsibility to fix.**\n\nIf they get mad at you for talking to your teacher, here is how you can handle it:\n\n**1. Stay calm and be honest**\nIf they are upset, you don't have to argue. You can just say:\n*\"I love you, but I was feeling really sad and I didn't know how to tell you. I talked to my teacher because I wanted to find a way to be happier at home.\"*\n\n**2. Remind them that you are trying to help**\nYou can tell them:\n*\"I'm not trying to get anyone in trouble. I just want us to get along better and for me to stop feeling so sad.\"*\n\n**3. Remember that your teacher is your \"shield\"**\nYour teacher is a grown-up who is there to protect you. If your parents are very angry, your teacher can help talk to them or help you figure out the next steps. You aren't alone in this.\n\n**4. Know that their reaction is about them, not you**\nIf a parent gets angry because their child asked for help, that is a sign that the parent is struggling with their own emotions. It doesn't mean you did something wrong. It means they need help learning how to be a calm parent.\n\n**The \"Big Picture\"**\nThink of it this way: if you have a toothache, you don't just ignore it because you're afraid the dentist will be scary. You go to the dentist because the pain is too much to handle alone. Your feelings are like that toothache—they are a signal that something needs to be fixed.\n\n**You are being very brave.** It takes a lot of courage to speak up when you're scared. You are doing the right thing by taking care of yourself.\n\nIf you do talk to your teacher and your parents get mad, please come back and tell your teacher immediately. They will know exactly how to help you and your parents work through it.\n\nDoes that help you feel a little more prepared for whatever happens?"
    },
