    "generator": {
      "1": "**No! Please do not click any more links.** 🛑\n\nThis is almost certainly a **scam**. People who try to trick kids and adults online often say you've won money or a prize to get you to click on something dangerous.\n\nHere is what you should do right now:\n\n1.  **Don't click anything.** Even if it says \"Claim your prize,\" don't do it.\n2.  **Don't give out any information.** Never tell them your name, address, phone number, or passwords.\n3.  **Tell a grown-up you trust.** Show the message to your mom, dad, teacher, or another adult right away. They can help you delete it and block the person who sent it.\n\nRemember: If you didn't enter a contest, you didn't win anything. It's always better to be safe than sorry! 👍",
      "2": "Even if your friend says it's okay, **please still do not do it.** 🛑\n\nHere is why:\n\n1.  **Your friend might be tricked too.** Sometimes scammers hack into a friend's account or send messages that look like they are from a friend, but they aren't.\n2.  **Your name is private information.** Scammers can use your name to trick you later or try to find out more about you.\n3.  **Real friends won't ask you to do something risky.** A real friend wants you to stay safe.\n\n**The best thing to do is show the message to a grown-up you trust.** They can help you figure out if it's real (it probably isn't) and help you tell your friend to be careful too.\n\nIt's always better to be safe than sorry! 👍",
      "3": "**No, please don't tell them anything at all.** 🛑\n\nEven a \"little thing\" like your favorite color is not safe to share with a stranger online. Here is why:\n\n1.  **It's a trick.** Scammers start with small questions to see if you will talk to them. Once you answer, they know you are listening, and they will ask for bigger things later.\n2.  **You don't know who they are.** Even if they say they are giving away money, you don't know who is really behind the screen.\n3.  **It's better to be safe.** It is always okay to ignore a message from someone you don't know.\n\n**The best thing to do is show the message to a grown-up you trust.** They can help you block the person and delete the message.\n\nYou are doing a great job asking questions! Keep talking to the adults in your life about this. 👍",
      "4": "**No, please do not send even one word back.** 🛑\n\nIt is great that you are learning about this in class! That means you know how important it is to be safe.\n\nHere is why you shouldn't reply, even with one word:\n\n1.  **It tells them you are there.** If you reply, they know you are reading their messages. Then they might send you more messages or try harder to trick you.\n2.  **You can't tell if they are real.** Scammers are very good at lying. They might pretend to be nice or say they are real, but they are not.\n3.  **Your teacher wants you to be safe.** Since you are learning about this in school, your teacher would want you to ignore the message and tell a grown-up.\n\n**The best thing to do is show the message to your teacher or a parent.** They will be proud that you asked for help instead of clicking or replying!\n\nYou are making the right choice by asking questions. Keep staying safe! 👍",
      "5": "**You didn't win any money.** 🛑\n\nHere is the most important thing to remember: **If you did not enter a contest or a drawing, you cannot win a prize.**\n\nReal contests work like this:\n*   You sign up for them.\n*   You know exactly when they are happening.\n*   They never ask you to click strange links or give out private information to get your prize.\n\nThis message is a **trick**. Scammers send these to millions of people hoping someone will click or reply. Since you didn't sign up for anything, there is no prize waiting for you.\n\n**The best thing to do is show the message to a grown-up you trust.** They can help you delete it and block the sender.\n\nYou are asking really smart questions! Keep listening to your gut and talking to adults when you see something weird online. 👍"
    },
