    "generator": {
      "1": "Bees make honey because it is their **food storage**!\n\nThink of it like this: bees are very busy during the spring and summer when there are lots of flowers. They collect nectar (a sugary liquid) from the flowers and turn it into honey. \n\nThey do this because in the winter, it gets too cold for them to fly, and there are no flowers to eat. So, they stay inside their hive and eat the honey they saved up to keep their energy and stay warm. \n\nIt's kind of like how you might pack a lunchbox for school so you have something to eat when you get hungry! 🍯🐝",
      "2": "It's actually a bit of both! Different animals have different ways of surviving the cold.\n\n**Some animals save food (like the bees!):**\n*   **Squirrels:** They are famous for this! They bury nuts and acorns all over the place in the fall so they can dig them up when they get hungry in the winter.\n*   **Beavers:** They collect branches and sticks and store them underwater near their lodge so they can snack on them without having to go far in the cold.\n\n**Some animals sleep through it (this is called hibernation):**\n*   **Bears:** They eat a ton of food in the fall to get very fat, and then they sleep for months. Their bodies use that stored fat for energy while they nap.\n*   **Groundhogs:** They curl up in their burrows and sleep very deeply until spring.\n\n**And some animals do a little bit of both!**\n*   **Chipmunks:** They hibernate, but they don't sleep the whole time. They wake up every few days to eat some of the food they stored in their burrows.\n\nSo, whether they are packing a pantry or taking a giant nap, they all have clever ways to make it until spring! 🐿️🐻",
